  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Mathematics<br><span>Glossary of Key Terms</span></h1>
    <p>Alphabetically sorted definitions of essential mathematical concepts, from foundational algebra to advanced university-level topics.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <div class="section-header">
      <h2>Definitions</h2>
    </div>

    <dl>
      <dt>Algebraic Group</dt>
      <dd>A set equipped with a binary operation satisfying closure, associativity, identity element, and inverse element properties. See also <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/abstract-algebra">Abstract Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt> Basis Vector</dt>
      <dd>A set of linearly independent vectors that span a vector space. Any vector in the space can be expressed as a unique linear combination of basis vectors. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Cauchy Sequence</dt>
      <dd>A sequence of elements in a metric space where elements become arbitrarily close as the sequence progresses. Fundamental in defining completeness of metric spaces. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/real-analysis">Real Analysis</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Chain Rule</dt>
      <dd>A formula for computing the derivative of a composite function: if y = f(g(x)), then dy/dx = f'(g(x)) · g'(x). Essential for differentiation of nested functions. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/multivariable-calculus">Multivariable Calculus</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Compactness</dt>
      <dd>A topological property where every open cover of a space has a finite subcover. Equivalent to sequential compactness in metric spaces. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/topology">Topology</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Complex Number</dt>
      <dd>A number of the form a + bi, where a and b are real numbers and i² = −1. Extends the real numbers to algebraically closed field. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/complex-analysis">Complex Analysis</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Convergence</dt>
      <dd>The property of a sequence or series approaching a limit. Includes pointwise, uniform, and conditional convergence types. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/real-analysis">Real Analysis</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Convex Set</dt>
      <dd>A set where, for any two points in the set, the entire line segment connecting them is also in the set. Fundamental in optimization and convex analysis.</dd>

      <dt>Diagonalization</dt>
      <dd>The process of finding an invertible matrix P and diagonal matrix D such that A = PDP⁻¹. A matrix is diagonalizable if it has n linearly independent eigenvectors. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Dimension</dt>
      <dd>The number of vectors in any basis for a vector space. All bases of a given vector space have the same number of elements. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Distribution</dt>
      <dd>A mathematical function describing the probability of different outcomes. Includes discrete (PMF), continuous (PDF), and mixed distributions. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/probability-and-statistics">Probability & Statistics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Eigenvalue</dt>
      <dd>A scalar λ such that Av = λv for a nonzero vector v. The characteristic equation det(A − λI) = 0 determines eigenvalues. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Eigenvector</dt>
      <dd>A nonzero vector v satisfying Av = λv for some scalar λ (eigenvalue). Represents directions invariant under the linear transformation.</dd>

      <dt>Field</dt>
      <dd>A set with addition and multiplication operations forming abelian groups, with distributivity. Examples include ℚ, ℝ, ℂ, and finite fields. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/abstract-algebra">Abstract Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Fourier Transform</dt>
      <dd>A mathematical transform decomposing functions into frequency components. Transforms a time-domain signal into its frequency-domain representation. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/multivariable-calculus">Multivariable Calculus</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Gaussian Elimination</dt>
      <dd>An algorithm for solving systems of linear equations using elementary row operations to reduce a matrix to row echelon form. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Group Homomorphism</dt>
      <dd>A function between two groups preserving the group operation: φ(ab) = φ(a)φ(b). Kernel and image are important structural properties. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/abstract-algebra">Abstract Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Hamiltonian</dt>
      <dd>A function H(q, p) representing the total energy of a system in terms of generalized coordinates and momenta. Fundamental in classical mechanics. See <a href="/topics/physics">Physics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Hypothesis Testing</dt>
      <dd>A statistical method for making decisions about population parameters based on sample data. Involves null and alternative hypotheses, test statistics, and p-values. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/probability-and-statistics">Probability & Statistics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Inner Product</dt>
      <dd>A function ⟨·,·⟩ on a vector space satisfying linearity, symmetry, and positive-definiteness. Generalizes dot product to abstract spaces. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Integration</dt>
      <dd>The process of finding areas, volumes, and accumulated quantities. Fundamental theorem connects integration to differentiation. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/multivariable-calculus">Multivariable Calculus</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Limit</dt>
      <dd>The value a function or sequence approaches as its argument approaches a given point. Foundation of calculus and analysis. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/real-analysis">Real Analysis</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Linear Independence</dt>
      <dd>A set of vectors is linearly independent if no vector can be expressed as a linear combination of the others. Determines dimension of vector spaces. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Matrix</dt>
      <dd>A rectangular array of numbers arranged in rows and columns. Represents linear transformations and systems of equations. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Metric Space</dt>
      <dd>A set equipped with a distance function d(x, y) satisfying non-negativity, symmetry, and triangle inequality. Generalizes Euclidean distance. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/real-analysis">Real Analysis</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Norm</dt>
      <dd>A function assigning a non-negative length to vectors, satisfying homogeneity and triangle inequality. Common examples include L¹, L², and L∞ norms. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Partial Derivative</dt>
      <dd>Derivative of a multivariable function with respect to one variable, holding others constant. Denoted ∂f/∂x. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/multivariable-calculus">Multivariable Calculus</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Prime Number</dt>
      <dd>An integer greater than 1 with no positive divisors other than 1 and itself. Fundamental building blocks of integers via unique factorization. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/number-theory">Number Theory</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Probability Density Function</dt>
      <dd>A function whose integral over a region gives the probability of a continuous random variable falling in that region. Total integral equals 1. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/probability-and-statistics">Probability & Statistics</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Quotient Space</dt>
      <dd>The set of equivalence classes of a vector space modulo a subspace. Fundamental in understanding structure of linear transformations. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Ring</dt>
      <dd>A set with addition and multiplication where addition forms an abelian group and multiplication is associative and distributive over addition. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/abstract-algebra">Abstract Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Scalar Field</dt>
      <dd>A function assigning a scalar to every point in space. Examples include temperature and pressure distributions in physics. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/multivariable-calculus">Multivariable Calculus</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Topology</dt>
      <dd>The study of properties preserved under continuous deformations (stretching, bending). Defines concepts like continuity, connectedness, and compactness. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/topology">Topology</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Uniform Convergence</dt>
      <dd>A type of convergence where a sequence of functions converges at the same rate over the entire domain. Preserves continuity and integrability properties. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/real-analysis">Real Analysis</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Vector Field</dt>
      <dd>A function assigning a vector to every point in space. Examples include velocity fields and electromagnetic fields. See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/multivariable-calculus">Multivariable Calculus</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Vector Space</dt>
      <dd>A set of elements (vectors) closed under addition and scalar multiplication, satisfying eight axioms (associativity, commutativity, identity, inverses, distributivity). See <a href="https://mathematics.wyattau.com/docs/mathematics/linear-algebra">Linear Algebra</a>.</dd>

      <dt>Wronskian</dt>
      <dd>The determinant of a matrix of functions and their derivatives. Used to determine linear independence of solutions to differential equations.</dd>
    </dl>
  </section>

